//@ run-pass
//@ expect-stdout: PASS

use darwin_pbpk::core::tissue_composition::{tc_pey_cell, tc_pey_intf, tc_pey_cnl, tc_pey_cnpl, tc_pey_cw, tc_pey_iw, tc_pey_ialb, tc_pey_pt_supported, tc_pey_pt_count, tc_peyret_plasma_nl, tc_peyret_plasma_npl, tc_peyret_plasma_alb}

// stdlib/darwin_pbpk/core/kp_methods.sio
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PBPK28 core module: the three published tissue:plasma partition (Kp)
// predictors, each carrying its own DECLARED APPLICABILITY DOMAIN and its own
// CALIBRATION STATUS, plus the ensemble rule that refuses -- fail-closed -- to
// form a model-form dispersion term when those two properties are not uniform
// across the admitted members.
//
// WHY THE DOMAIN AND CALIBRATION FLAGS ARE PART OF THE DATA, NOT A COMMENT
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Measured 2026-10-31, muscle, unbound tissue:plasma partition Kpu:
//
//   drug                    R-R      Schmitt(raw)   Poulin-Theil    spread
//   haloperidol (strong B)  80.90        796.29           8.69       91.7x
//   midazolam (weak base)   35.16         32.10          31.00        1.1x
//   neutral, logP 3         25.42         16.30          22.35        1.6x
//
// In domain the three agree to 1.1x.  That spread is NOT by itself a Type B
// standard uncertainty: the members read the same composition table and the
// same drug inputs, so they are correlated and their spread can UNDERSTATE the
// uncertainty; turning it into a u needs a stated distribution, weights and
// dependence model, which this module does not supply.  Out of domain they span
// ~92x, and that number is not an uncertainty either -- it is the distance to
// the members that do not apply.  Poulin-
// Theil has no acidic-phospholipid term at all; predicting a strong base with
// it was the stated motivation for Rodgers-Rowland.  So a dispersion computed
// over a non-uniform ensemble reports a domain error as an uncertainty.
//
// The second, independent trap is calibration.  The Schmitt method AS SHIPPED
// in the reference tool (httk) applies, by default, a fitted per-tissue log-log
// shrinkage -- Pearce et al. 2017 -- on top of the mechanistic core:
//
//   Kpu_corr = 10^(a + b*log10(Kpu_raw * fu_p)) / fu_p ,   b < 1 everywhere
//
// For haloperidol this moves predicted Vss from 21350 L to 2258 L against
// 1216 L observed (17.6x -> 1.86x); in adipose, brain and skin the mechanistic
// part supplies the ORDERING and the regression supplies the LEVEL (factor
// 0.03-0.04x).  Pearce was fitted to in vivo partition data, so a dispersion
// taken between an uncalibrated member and a calibrated one MAY share data with
// whatever it is later evaluated against -- the calibration / discrepancy
// confounding of Brynjarsdottir & O'Hagan.  Whether Pearce 2017's calibration
// set actually includes the drugs evaluated here has NOT been checked, and a
// calibrated model is not unusable by that fact alone.  kp_ensemble admits only
// members of one calibration status as a conservative default, not because the
// mixing has been shown to be invalid in this case.
//
// ORACLE VALIDATION.  The Schmitt predictors here reproduce
// httk::predict_partitioning_schmitt (httk 2.7.4) to 1.000x on 10 of 11
// tissues in both modes; the one 0.4% deviation was gut, and it is explained:
// httk normalises Fcell and Fint by their sum, and gut is the only tissue whose
// sum is not 1.000 (0.900 + 0.096 = 0.996).  That normalisation is applied
// below.  Poulin-Theil has NO executable oracle -- httk implements only Schmitt
// -- so it is faithful to the primary source on the dominant terms but its
// interstitial term is a reconstruction.  See kpm_pt_oracle_validated().
//
// SCOPE OF THIS MODULE.  Muscle only.  Muscle is 33.89 L of the 66.6 L of
// tissue in the Rodgers-Rowland Table VI human volumes and dominates Vss, so
// it is the right single-tissue witness.  Extending to all 14 organs requires
// an acidic-phospholipid field [AP-] in core/tissue_composition.sio, which that
// table does not yet carry.  Composition constants are therefore inline and
// each is attributed below, rather than read from a table that cannot yet
// express the Rodgers-Rowland parameterisation.
//
// Per dissertation convention this stdlib module stays PLAIN f64; the epistemic
// wrapping (Knowledge<f64, eps>) is demonstrated in the witness tests.
//
// References:
//   Rodgers T & Rowland M (2007) Pharm Res 24:918-933 -- Eqs. 1-6, Tables II/VII
//   Schmitt W (2008) Toxicol In Vitro 22:457-467 (+ corrigendum) -- Eqs. 1-19
//   Pearce RG et al. (2017) J Pharmacokinet Pharmacodyn 44:549-565 -- regression
//   Peyret T, Poulin P & Krishnan K (2010) Toxicol Appl Pharmacol 249:197-207
//   Poulin P & Theil F-P (2002) J Pharm Sci 91:129-156
//   Brynjarsdottir J & O'Hagan A (2014) Inverse Problems 30:114007
//
// PRIOR ART -- what this module does NOT claim
// --------------------------------------------
// The critique encoded in the domain and calibration gates is NOT original to
// this work, and the gates are built on it rather than asserting it:
//
//   Korzekwa K & Nagar S (2016) Pharm Res 34:529-534,
//   doi 10.1007/s11095-016-2089-8 -- argue current PBPK models are
//   "mechanistic but a posteriori and semi-empirical". They state that
//   back-calculating Ka_AP from the blood:plasma ratio is CIRCULAR ("K_aAP is
//   calculated from erythrocyte partitioning, which is itself highly
//   correlated with tissue partitioning, and K_aAP is then used to calculate
//   tissue partitioning"); that errors in fu_p "will largely cancel" as a
//   consequence; that the neutral-phospholipid 70%-water / 30%-lipid split
//   rests on a single HLB value for lecithin whose reported range is 3-9; and
//   that the vegetable-oil:water correlation used for adipose has an average
//   fold error of 5.1 over n=32 drugs. They also note that tuning parameters
//   to recover an observed Vss makes the model empirical and destroys the
//   tissue-specific information.
//
//   Yau E, Olivares-Morales A & Parrott NJ (2020) AAPS J 22:41,
//   doi 10.1208/s12248-020-0418-7 -- global sensitivity analysis of the
//   Rodgers-Rowland model. The local sensitivities quoted in this module are a
//   special case of that published analysis.
//
//   Berry LM et al. (2011) Drug Metab Dispos, doi 10.1124/dmd.111.040766 --
//   Rodgers-Rowland overpredicts Vss by more than fourfold for logP > 3.5,
//   with some compounds near 100-fold.
//
//   Coutinho AL, Cristofoletti R & Wu F (2024) Pharm Res 41:1121-1138,
//   doi 10.1007/s11095-024-03703-4 -- six Vss methods under logP uncertainty;
//   the Rodgers-Rowland variants are the most logP-sensitive and overpredict.
//
//   Lautz LS, Dorne JLCM & Punt A (2024) Toxicol Lett 398:140-149,
//   doi 10.1016/j.toxlet.2024.06.012 -- partition-coefficient methods compared
//   across species, stratified by ionisation state.
//
//   Farahat M, Flaherty DT & Fox Z (2026) npj Syst Biol Appl 12(1),
//   doi 10.1038/s41540-026-00739-7 -- four PBPK models validated against 1854
//   datapoints, then perturbed; they report model disagreement concentrated in
//   "lipophilic, protonated molecules" and identify the property and parameter
//   interactions driving it. That is the same localisation this module's
//   domain gate enforces, measured at far larger scale.
//
//   Hartung N & Huisinga W, PAGE 32 (2024) Abstr. 11096 -- unify the two
//   Rodgers routes and question interpolation across the pKa 7.0 jump that
//   this module still reproduces (see the note in kpm_rodgers_rowland).
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES, AND ITS LIMITS.  It implements a refusal: kpm_ensemble
// declines to form a dispersion statistic when an admitted member is outside
// its declared domain or calibration status is not uniform.  Three limits:
//   - it is a RUNTIME check over flags declared by the caller, not a static
//     guarantee of the type system;
//   - it cannot authenticate those flags -- a caller who sets has_ma = true on
//     an imputed membrane affinity is admitted (witnessed by T8 below);
//   - whether existing tools (PK-Sim, Simcyp, GastroPlus) already gate on
//     applicability domain has not been checked, so no novelty is claimed.
//
// Author: Demetrios Chiuratto Agourakis

use math::pure::{exp, ln, log10}

// ============================================================================
// Shared drug inputs
// ============================================================================
// One input record feeds every member, so that any residual disagreement is
// attributable to the methods and not to each paper's own input curation.
// Note that this does NOT make the members comparable by itself: measured
// membrane affinity (log_ma) is read only by Schmitt and has no Rodgers-Rowland
// analogue.  Measured 2026-10-31 at muscle, haloperidol.  These are
// SEMI-ELASTICITIES of Kpu, d(ln Kpu)/d(x) per unit of x -- not elasticities,
// and not of Kp:
//     logP    R-R  0.49   Schmitt  0.03
//     pKa     R-R -0.47   Schmitt -0.05
//     fu_p    R-R -3.95   Schmitt -0.001
//     R / MA  R-R  1.72   Schmitt  2.26
// For the quantity that enters Vss the picture changes: the ELASTICITY of Kp
// (= fu_p * Kpu) to fu_p is about -0.03 for Rodgers-Rowland, because the
// blood:plasma back-calculation cancels fu_p (Korzekwa & Nagar 2016), and about
// +1.00 for Schmitt.  The members depend on different inputs; that alone does
// NOT show that they estimate different quantities.

struct KpInputs {
    log_p:   f64,   // n-octanol:water log P of the NEUTRAL species
    log_ma:  f64,   // log membrane affinity; read only when has_ma is true
    has_ma:  bool,  // true only when log_ma is MEASURED, never imputed from logP
    pka:     f64,   // monoprotic basic pKa (0.0 for a neutral compound)
    is_base: bool,
    fu_p:    f64,   // fraction unbound in plasma
    rb:      f64,   // blood:plasma ratio
    mw:      f64    // g/mol
}

// A single member's verdict.  Carrying in_domain and calibrated alongside the
// number is the whole point: a bare f64 cannot be audited.
struct KpMember {
    kpu:        f64,
    in_domain:  bool,
    calibrated: bool,   // output passed through a correction fitted to data
    code:       i32     // 0 = R-R, 1 = Schmitt raw, 2 = Schmitt+Pearce, 3 = Poulin-Theil
}

// ============================================================================
// Physiological constants (muscle + blood), each attributed
// ============================================================================
pub fn kpm_ph_plasma() -> f64 { return 7.40 }
pub fn kpm_ph_rbc()    -> f64 { return 7.22 }   // R-R 2007 Tab. VII
pub fn kpm_hematocrit()-> f64 { return 0.45 }
// Schmitt / httk tissue.data, muscle intracellular pH.  Flagged: this 6.81 is
// biased acid relative to modern in vivo 31P-MRS (7.08); see the pH card.
pub fn kpm_ph_cell_schmitt() -> f64 { return 6.81 }
// Rodgers-Rowland intracellular pH (their Tab. VII convention).
pub fn kpm_ph_cell_rr() -> f64 { return 7.00 }
// Peyret 2010 cell pH for the Poulin-Theil algorithm.
pub fn kpm_ph_cell_pt() -> f64 { return 7.00 }
// ion-permeability ratio alpha (Schmitt): charged species membrane access
pub fn kpm_alpha() -> f64 { return 0.001 }

// ============================================================================
// PER-ORGAN COMPOSITION  (added 2026-11-08) -- ONE SOURCE, httk 2.7.4
// ============================================================================
// Every field below is read from httk 2.7.4 data/Tables.RData, tissue.data,
// Species == "Human", plus the per-tissue Pearce regression coefficients from
// pearce2017regression (adj.fup.intercept / adj.fup.slope).  Nothing here is
// interpolated, averaged or carried over from a neighbouring tissue.
//
// Using ONE composition source across all members is deliberate and it is a
// change of meaning, not just of coverage: on muscle the ensemble mixed R-R
// Tab. VII with httk (the -11.52% [AP-] discrepancy recorded in
// tissue_composition.sio), so part of its dispersion was source disagreement
// rather than model form.  With a single source the dispersion is closer to
// model form alone -- and, by the same token, composition perturbations should
// become MORE blind, because every member now reads the same numbers.  That is
// a prediction, pinned by test, not a hope.
//
// TWO ORGANS DO NOT CLOSE, and both are declared rather than smoothed:
//  - bone: FWc 0.26 + FLc 0.02 + FPc 0.21 = 0.49, a 51% shortfall.  The
//    missing volume is mineral, for which the Schmitt cell-fraction scheme has
//    no category (density 1.976 g/cm^3 against ~1.0 for soft tissue confirms
//    it).  Bone is therefore REFUSED, not modelled: coverage is 10 of 14, not
//    11.  Pinned by test.
//  - gut: Fcell 0.900 + Fint 0.096 = 0.996.  httk normalises the pair by its
//    sum, which is the documented origin of the one 0.4% deviation in the
//    oracle validation.  The normalisation is reproduced here rather than
//    corrected, so the oracle comparison stays meaningful.
struct SchmittComp {
    f_cell: f64,
    f_int:  f64,
    f_wc:   f64,
    f_lc:   f64,
    f_pc:   f64,
    fn_lc:  f64,
    fn_plc: f64,
    fa_plc: f64,
    ph_c:   f64,
    pe_i:   f64,   // Pearce adj.fup.intercept
    pe_s:   f64    // Pearce adj.fup.slope
}

// 14-organ index, matching tissue_composition.sio:
//   0=blood, 1=liver, 2=kidney, 3=brain, 4=heart, 5=lung, 6=muscle,
//   7=adipose, 8=gut, 9=skin, 10=bone, 11=spleen, 12=pancreas, 13=other
// Supported: 1-9 and 11.  Refused: 0 (plasma is not a cell), 10 (bone does
// not close), 12 and 13 (absent from httk).
pub fn kpm_organ_supported(organ: i32) -> bool {
    if organ <= 0  { return false }
    if organ == 10 { return false }
    if organ >= 12 { return false }
    return true
}

pub fn kpm_organ_count() -> i32 { return 10 }

// Why organ 10 is refused, as a datum rather than a comment.
pub fn kpm_organ_bone_closure() -> f64 { return 0.49 }

fn kpm_organ_comp(organ: i32) -> SchmittComp {
    if organ == 1  { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.77000, f_int: 0.23000, f_wc: 0.72440, f_lc: 0.092310, f_pc: 0.18330, fn_lc: 0.724400, fn_plc: 0.225100, fa_plc: 0.050510, ph_c: 7.23, pe_i: 0.475, pe_s: 0.621 } }
    if organ == 2  { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.83500, f_int: 0.16500, f_wc: 0.76610, f_lc: 0.064070, f_pc: 0.16980, fn_lc: 0.637500, fn_plc: 0.294000, fa_plc: 0.068530, ph_c: 7.22, pe_i: 0.452, pe_s: 0.673 } }
    if organ == 3  { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.99500, f_int: 0.00500, f_wc: 0.80430, f_lc: 0.112300, f_pc: 0.08341, fn_lc: 0.373400, fn_plc: 0.458800, fa_plc: 0.167800, ph_c: 7.10, pe_i: 0.0 - 0.167, pe_s: 0.543 } }
    if organ == 4  { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.75000, f_int: 0.25000, f_wc: 0.69570, f_lc: 0.137500, f_pc: 0.16680, fn_lc: 0.890100, fn_plc: 0.079010, fa_plc: 0.030900, ph_c: 7.10, pe_i: 0.116, pe_s: 0.683 } }
    if organ == 5  { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.80000, f_int: 0.20000, f_wc: 0.80380, f_lc: 0.013160, f_pc: 0.18300, fn_lc: 0.300000, fn_plc: 0.560000, fa_plc: 0.140000, ph_c: 6.60, pe_i: 0.087, pe_s: 0.866 } }
    if organ == 6  { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.85000, f_int: 0.15000, f_wc: 0.79910, f_lc: 0.023870, f_pc: 0.17700, fn_lc: 0.539400, fn_plc: 0.381700, fa_plc: 0.078840, ph_c: 6.81, pe_i: 0.0 - 0.022, pe_s: 0.658 } }
    if organ == 7  { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.86000, f_int: 0.14000, f_wc: 0.02002, f_lc: 0.934000, f_pc: 0.04601, fn_lc: 0.936100, fn_plc: 0.056070, fa_plc: 0.007859, ph_c: 7.10, pe_i: 0.0 - 0.325, pe_s: 0.574 } }
    if organ == 8  { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.90000, f_int: 0.09600, f_wc: 0.78000, f_lc: 0.070000, f_pc: 0.15000, fn_lc: 0.690000, fn_plc: 0.260000, fa_plc: 0.050000, ph_c: 7.00, pe_i: 0.143, pe_s: 0.764 } }
    if organ == 9  { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.40000, f_int: 0.60000, f_wc: 0.42700, f_lc: 0.283100, f_pc: 0.28980, fn_lc: 0.359900, fn_plc: 0.501900, fa_plc: 0.138200, ph_c: 7.00, pe_i: 0.0 - 0.090, pe_s: 0.566 } }
    if organ == 11 { return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.74500, f_int: 0.25500, f_wc: 0.76990, f_lc: 0.039460, f_pc: 0.19070, fn_lc: 0.534100, fn_plc: 0.393000, fa_plc: 0.072870, ph_c: 7.00, pe_i: 0.034, pe_s: 0.765 } }
    // Refused organs: a zeroed record, never reached by a supported caller.
    return SchmittComp { f_cell: 0.0, f_int: 0.0, f_wc: 0.0, f_lc: 0.0, f_pc: 0.0,
                         fn_lc: 0.0, fn_plc: 0.0, fa_plc: 0.0, ph_c: 0.0, pe_i: 0.0, pe_s: 0.0 }
}

fn kpm_pow10(x: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic { exp(x * 2.302585092994046) }

fn kpm_abs(x: f64) -> f64 { if x < 0.0 { 0.0 - x } else { x } }

fn kpm_sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div {
    if x <= 0.0 { return 0.0 }
    var y = x
    var i = 0
    while i < 14 { y = (y + x / y) * 0.5; i = i + 1 }
    y
}

// Neutral fraction of a monoprotic base at pH (Henderson-Hasselbalch).
fn kpm_f_neutral(pka: f64, ph: f64, is_base: bool) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    if !is_base { return 1.0 }
    1.0 / (1.0 + kpm_pow10(pka - ph))
}

// Schmitt D_ow: octanol:water distribution with charged-species access alpha.
// Generalises Schmitt Eqs. 13/14 -- D = P*(f_n*(1-alpha) + alpha).
fn kpm_dow(pow_v: f64, f_n: f64) -> f64 {
    pow_v * (f_n * (1.0 - kpm_alpha()) + kpm_alpha())
}

// ============================================================================
// B:P CONSISTENCY OF THE STRONG-BASE ROUTE  (audit 2026-11-02)
// ============================================================================
// The strong-base route does not fit Ka: it SOLVES for it from the measured
// blood:plasma ratio, via the blood-cell Kpu that rb implies
//   Kpu_bc = (rb - (1 - Ht)) / (Ht * fu_p)
// and then inverts R-R Eq. 1 over blood-cell composition.  That inversion has
// a non-negative solution only when rb is large enough to leave room for a
// phospholipid term after the water and lipid terms are paid for.  Below that
// threshold the algebra returns Ka < 0, which is not a small numerical
// nuisance: it says the measured rb is INCONSISTENT with the blood-cell
// composition table, so no R-R Eq. 1 blood cell can reproduce it.
//
// The previous code clamped Ka to 0.0 and returned a number anyway.  Measured
// 2026-11-02 (scratchpad/audit1/bp1.cpp, bp2.cpp): where the clamp fires the
// model's own predicted B:P stops matching its input.  For a strong base with
// logP 3.2, pKa 9.4, fu_p 0.73 and rb = 0.56, the clamped model implies
// rb = 0.873483 -- an error of +0.3135, i.e. +56%, returned silently with
// in_domain still true.  That is the same defect class Hartung & Huisinga
// (PAGE 32, 2024, Abstr. 11096) found in the Lukacova variant: predicted B:P
// differs from input B:P.  There it is structural; here it was conditional and
// fail-OPEN, which is worse in a module whose whole claim is fail-closed.
//
// Above the threshold the round-trip is exact.  Measured round-trip error on
// the dissertation set: haloperidol 0.0, venlafaxine -1.11e-16, ODV 0.0,
// clozapine +1.11e-16.  So the back-calculation itself is sound; only the
// clamp was not.
//
// MARGINS MEASURED (rb, critical rb, margin):
//   haloperidol  1.080  0.6946  +35.7%
//   venlafaxine  1.000  0.8735  +12.7%   <- thinnest in the set
//   ODV          1.000  0.8523  +14.8%
//   clozapine    0.860  0.6010  +30.1%
// The drug this dissertation is about has the smallest margin to a silent
// failure, and the input that sets that margin -- rb -- is the one that was
// never measured for it (rb = 1.00 is an assumption, see SIO-545).  Sweeping
// fu_p from 0.70 to 0.95 never crosses the threshold at rb = 1.00, so fu_p is
// not the lever: rb is.  If venlafaxine's true rb is below 0.8735 the old code
// would have returned a Kpu built on Ka = 0 -- no phospholipid binding at all
// for a strong base -- with no signal.
//
// Returns the signed margin rb - rb_critical.  Positive means a non-negative
// Ka exists.  The weak-base/acid route never reads rb, so it is reported as
// trivially consistent with a margin of 0.0 rather than a false number.
pub fn kpm_rr_bp_margin(d: KpInputs) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let strong = if d.is_base { d.pka >= 7.0 } else { false }
    if !strong { return 0.0 }
    if d.fu_p <= 0.0 { return 0.0 }
    let p  = kpm_pow10(d.log_p)
    let ht = kpm_hematocrit()
    let y  = kpm_pow10(d.pka - kpm_ph_plasma())
    let z  = kpm_pow10(d.pka - kpm_ph_rbc())
    let lip_bc = p * 0.0012 + (0.3 * p + 0.7) * 0.0033
    let kpu_crit = (1.0 + z) / (1.0 + y) * 0.63 + lip_bc / (1.0 + y)
    let rb_crit = ht * d.fu_p * kpu_crit + (1.0 - ht)
    return d.rb - rb_crit
}

// True when the strong-base route can solve for a non-negative Ka from the
// declared rb.  False is a REFUSAL, not a warning: the ensemble reports it as
// refusal code 4 so that it is distinguishable from a structural domain miss.
pub fn kpm_rr_bp_consistent(d: KpInputs) -> bool with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let strong = if d.is_base { d.pka >= 7.0 } else { false }
    if !strong { return true }
    return kpm_rr_bp_margin(d) >= 0.0
}

// ============================================================================
// MEMBER 0 -- Rodgers & Rowland 2007, strong-base route (Eqs. 1 + 3), muscle
// ============================================================================
// Composition: R-R 2007 Tab. VII, human muscle and blood cells.
//   muscle  extracellular water 0.079, intracellular water 0.666,
//           neutral lipid 0.022, neutral phospholipid 0.0078, [AP-] 2.42 mg/g
//   blood cells  water 0.63, neutral lipid 0.0012, phospholipid 0.0033,
//                [AP-] 0.57 mg/g
//
// The acidic-phospholipid association constant Ka is NOT a free parameter: it
// is back-calculated from the blood-cell partition implied by the measured
// blood:plasma ratio.  A uniform error in [AP-] therefore CANCELS EXACTLY
// between that calibration and the tissue prediction (Ka scales as 1/AP_rbc
// while the tissue term scales as Ka*AP_tissue).  Verified numerically:
// perturbing both [AP-] values by +10% moves ln Kpu by 0.0000.  So Rodgers-
// Rowland depends on [AP-] only through the tissue / blood-cell RATIO.  That is
// an invariance of the parameterisation, not evidence of a hidden error.  Its
// practical consequence: a uniform error in [AP-] leaves Kpu unchanged, only a
// DIFFERENTIAL error between blood cells and tissue moves it, and extending to
// more organs needs sourced RATIOS rather than sourced absolutes.
// in_domain is the CONJUNCTION of two independent conditions, and the caller
// can tell them apart through kpm_rr_in_domain / kpm_rr_bp_consistent:
//   - structural domain (small molecule, MW < 700, fu_p > 0);
//   - B:P consistency, i.e. the strong-base route can solve a non-negative Ka
//     from the declared rb.  Before 2026-11-02 this second condition was not
//     checked and a negative Ka was clamped to 0.0, so the member stayed
//     in_domain while the model's own B:P no longer matched its input.
pub fn kpm_rodgers_rowland(d: KpInputs) -> KpMember with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let ok = if kpm_rr_in_domain(d) { kpm_rr_bp_consistent(d) } else { false }
    return KpMember { kpu: kpm_rr_kpu_ph(d, kpm_ph_cell_rr()),
                      in_domain: ok, calibrated: false, code: 0 }
}

// The Kpu body with the intracellular pH passed in, so that kpm_blindness can
// perturb a SHARED input across every member.  kpm_rodgers_rowland calls it
// with this method's native pH, so the oracle-validated numbers are unchanged.
fn kpm_rr_kpu_ph(d: KpInputs, ph_c: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    kpm_rr_kpu_full(d, ph_c, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
}

// s_nl, s_np, s_ap are RELATIVE multipliers on the neutral-lipid,
// neutral-phospholipid and acidic-phospholipid content, applied to BOTH the
// tissue and the blood-cell figures so that a "shared" composition error can be
// perturbed the way kpm_blindness needs. All three are 1.0 on the normal path.
fn kpm_rr_kpu_full(d: KpInputs, ph_c: f64, s_nl: f64, s_np: f64, s_ap: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let p  = kpm_pow10(d.log_p)
    let ht = kpm_hematocrit()

    // ionisation ratios X (cell), Y (plasma), Z (blood cell)
    let x = kpm_pow10(d.pka - ph_c)
    let y = kpm_pow10(d.pka - kpm_ph_plasma())
    let z = kpm_pow10(d.pka - kpm_ph_rbc())

    // lipid term P*f_nl + (0.3P + 0.7)*f_np   (R-R 2007 Eq. 1 lipid group)
    let lip_mus = p * (0.022 * s_nl)   + (0.3 * p + 0.7) * (0.0078 * s_np)
    let lip_bc  = p * (0.0012 * s_nl)  + (0.3 * p + 0.7) * (0.0033 * s_np)
    let ap_mus  = 2.42 * s_ap
    let ap_bc   = 0.57 * s_ap

    // ROUTE SELECTION.  Rodgers-Rowland use two different mechanisms and the
    // choice is theirs, not a tuning knob: Eq. 1 (association with acidic
    // phospholipid, Ka back-calculated from blood cells) for STRONG bases,
    // pKa >= 7; Eq. 2 (association with albumin, tissue:plasma protein ratio
    // 0.064 for muscle) for acids, neutrals, weak bases and zwitterions.
    // Applying the strong-base route to a weak base inflates Kpu, because the
    // cation-phospholipid term is then driven by an ionised fraction that does
    // not exist at intracellular pH.
    // KNOWN LIMITATION -- the pKa 7.0 route switch is a DISCONTINUITY, and it
    // has a published fix that is not applied here.
    //
    // Hartung N & Huisinga W, PAGE 32 (2024) Abstr. 11096, "A unified Rodgers
    // et al. model for predicting tissue partitioning reveals the need for a
    // theoretical derivation of the Lukacova et al. (Simulations Plus) model",
    // unify the two routes into a single model in which the drug dissolves in
    // extra- and intracellular water, binds interstitial proteins in its
    // neutral and anionic forms, and binds cellular acidic phospholipids in its
    // cationic form. Their unified form gives a SMOOTH dependence of Kpu on pKa
    // and reproduces the input B:P consistently; they question the validity of
    // interpolating across the pKa 7.0 jump that the two-route formulation
    // creates.
    //
    // This matters for the dissertation's own drug set, which straddles the
    // boundary: venlafaxine pKa 9.4 and ODV 9.5 take the strong-base route
    // while midazolam at 6.01 takes the weak-base one. Worse, kpm_pt_in_domain
    // gates Poulin-Theil at the SAME pKa 7.0, so two separate decisions sit on
    // one arbitrary threshold and neither is smooth in pKa.
    //
    // The two-route form is kept for now because it is what Rodgers & Rowland
    // published and what the oracle-validated reference values correspond to;
    // replacing it with the unified model would move every parity figure and
    // belongs in its own change, against the primary source rather than an
    // abstract. Until then, treat any Kpu for a drug with pKa near 7 as
    // carrying an undeclared discontinuity, not as a smooth prediction.
    let strong = if d.is_base { d.pka >= 7.0 } else { false }

    let iw_term = (1.0 + x) / (1.0 + y) * 0.666 + 0.079

    if strong {
        // Kpu of blood cells implied by the measured blood:plasma ratio
        let kpu_bc = (d.rb - (1.0 - ht)) / (ht * d.fu_p)
        var ka = (kpu_bc - (1.0 + z) / (1.0 + y) * 0.63 - lip_bc / (1.0 + y)) * ((1.0 + y) / (ap_bc * z))
        if ka < 0.0 { ka = 0.0 }
        return iw_term + ka * ap_mus * x / (1.0 + y) + lip_mus / (1.0 + y)
    }

    // Eq. 2 -- albumin route.  The albumin association term is itself
    // back-calculated from the measured plasma unbound fraction, so like Ka it
    // is not fitted: KaPR*[PR]_p = 1/fu - 1 - P*f_nl_plasma/(1+Y).
    // Plasma neutral lipid 0.0032 and phospholipid 0.0021 (R-R 2007 Tab. VII);
    // muscle:plasma albumin ratio 0.064.
    let lip_pl = p * (0.0032 * s_nl) + (0.3 * p + 0.7) * (0.0021 * s_np)
    var ka_pr = 1.0 / d.fu_p - 1.0 - lip_pl / (1.0 + y)
    if ka_pr < 0.0 { ka_pr = 0.0 }
    return iw_term + ka_pr * 0.064 + lip_mus / (1.0 + y)
}

// Declared domain: Rodgers-Rowland was derived and validated for small
// molecules -- the 2005/2006/2007 series covers strong bases, acids, neutrals
// and zwitterions -- with MW below roughly 700 g/mol.  Peptides, glycopeptides
// and inorganic ions fall outside it.
pub fn kpm_rr_in_domain(d: KpInputs) -> bool {
    if d.mw > 700.0 { return false }
    if d.fu_p <= 0.0 { return false }
    return true
}

// ============================================================================
// MEMBER 1 -- Schmitt 2008, mechanistic core only (no fitted correction)
// ============================================================================
// Composition: httk 2.7.4 tissue.data, human muscle.  Verified tissue by
// tissue that FWc + FLc + FPc = 1 (fractions of CELL volume) and that
// Fn_Lc + Fn_PLc + Fa_PLc = 1 (fractions of LIPID), so the phospholipid
// fractions are multiplied by the total lipid fraction FLc:
//   Fcell 0.850  Fint 0.150
//   FWc 0.79910  FLc 0.02387  FPc 0.17700
//   Fn_Lc 0.5394  Fn_PLc 0.38170  Fa_PLc 0.078840
//
// Eq. 19 gives the protein:water partition KP = 0.163 + 0.0221*Kn_PL.
// Eqs. 17/18 are published with their LABELS SWAPPED -- the equation printed
// for neutral phospholipid is the acidic one and vice versa -- and the swap is
// not in the corrigendum.  The physically coherent form is used here; copying
// the printed labels costs 3.0x in muscle and 5.6x in brain for haloperidol.
pub fn kpm_schmitt_raw(d: KpInputs) -> KpMember with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let in_dom = kpm_schmitt_in_domain(d)
    let kpu = kpm_schmitt_core(d)
    return KpMember { kpu: kpu, in_domain: in_dom, calibrated: false, code: 1 }
}

fn kpm_schmitt_core(d: KpInputs) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    kpm_schmitt_core_ph(d, kpm_ph_cell_schmitt())
}

fn kpm_schmitt_core_ph(d: KpInputs, ph_c: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    kpm_schmitt_core_full(d, ph_c, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
}

fn kpm_schmitt_core_full(d: KpInputs, ph_c: f64, s_nl: f64, s_np: f64, s_ap: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let pow_v = kpm_pow10(d.log_p)
    let kn_pl = kpm_pow10(d.log_ma)

    let f_n = kpm_f_neutral(d.pka, ph_c, d.is_base)
    let f_c = 1.0 - f_n
    let kn_l  = kpm_dow(pow_v, f_n)
    let ka_pl = kn_pl * (f_n + 20.0 * f_c)      // cation affinity for acidic PL
    let kp_pr = 0.163 + 0.0221 * kn_pl          // Eq. 19

    // cellular inverse-unbound-fraction (Eq. 7)
    let inv_cell = 0.79910
                   + kn_l  * 0.02387 * 0.5394   * s_nl
                   + kn_pl * 0.02387 * 0.38170  * s_np
                   + ka_pl * 0.02387 * 0.078840 * s_ap
                   + kp_pr * 0.17700
    // interstitial (Eq. 4)
    let inv_int = 0.94 + 0.37 * (1.0 / d.fu_p - 0.945)

    // pH-gradient factor kappa (Eq. 10/11 generalised)
    let f_n_p = kpm_f_neutral(d.pka, kpm_ph_plasma(), d.is_base)
    let num_k = f_n_p + kpm_alpha() * (1.0 - f_n_p)
    let den_k = f_n   + kpm_alpha() * (1.0 - f_n)
    let kappa = num_k / den_k

    // httk normalises Fcell and Fint by their sum; for muscle the sum is 1.000
    let f_total = 0.850 + 0.150
    let f_cell = 0.850 / f_total
    let f_int  = 0.150 / f_total
    return f_int * inv_int + kappa * f_cell * inv_cell
}

// The Schmitt core generalised over the 14-organ index.  Structurally
// identical to kpm_schmitt_core_full; the muscle constants are replaced by a
// lookup.  Test T13 pins that organ 6 through this path reproduces
// kpm_schmitt_core_full bit for bit, which is what certifies the table.
fn kpm_schmitt_core_organ(d: KpInputs, organ: i32, ph_c: f64,
                          s_nl: f64, s_np: f64, s_ap: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let c = kpm_organ_comp(organ)
    let pow_v = kpm_pow10(d.log_p)
    let kn_pl = kpm_pow10(d.log_ma)

    let f_n = kpm_f_neutral(d.pka, ph_c, d.is_base)
    let f_c = 1.0 - f_n
    let kn_l  = kpm_dow(pow_v, f_n)
    let ka_pl = kn_pl * (f_n + 20.0 * f_c)
    let kp_pr = 0.163 + 0.0221 * kn_pl

    let inv_cell = c.f_wc
                   + kn_l  * c.f_lc * c.fn_lc  * s_nl
                   + kn_pl * c.f_lc * c.fn_plc * s_np
                   + ka_pl * c.f_lc * c.fa_plc * s_ap
                   + kp_pr * c.f_pc
    let inv_int = 0.94 + 0.37 * (1.0 / d.fu_p - 0.945)

    let f_n_p = kpm_f_neutral(d.pka, kpm_ph_plasma(), d.is_base)
    let num_k = f_n_p + kpm_alpha() * (1.0 - f_n_p)
    let den_k = f_n   + kpm_alpha() * (1.0 - f_n)
    let kappa = num_k / den_k

    // httk normalises the pair by its sum; for gut the sum is 0.996, not 1.0.
    let f_total = c.f_cell + c.f_int
    let f_cell = c.f_cell / f_total
    let f_int  = c.f_int  / f_total
    return f_int * inv_int + kappa * f_cell * inv_cell
}

// Schmitt, uncalibrated, at an organ's own cellular pH.  in_domain carries the
// organ gate as well as the measured-log-MA requirement: an unsupported organ
// is a refusal, not a number.
pub fn kpm_schmitt_raw_organ(d: KpInputs, organ: i32) -> KpMember with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let c = kpm_organ_comp(organ)
    let ok = if kpm_organ_supported(organ) { d.has_ma } else { false }
    return KpMember { kpu: kpm_schmitt_core_organ(d, organ, c.ph_c, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0),
                      in_domain: ok, calibrated: false, code: 1 }
}

// Schmitt with the Pearce 2017 per-tissue regression, which httk applies by
// default: Kpu_corr = 10^(a + b*log10(Kpu_raw*fu_p)) / fu_p, with (a, b) the
// organ's own adj.fup.intercept and adj.fup.slope.  b < 1 in all ten organs,
// so the correction always shrinks a large prediction toward the data.
pub fn kpm_schmitt_pearce_organ(d: KpInputs, organ: i32) -> KpMember with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let c = kpm_organ_comp(organ)
    let ok = if kpm_organ_supported(organ) { d.has_ma } else { false }
    let raw = kpm_schmitt_core_organ(d, organ, c.ph_c, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
    let arg = raw * d.fu_p
    if arg <= 0.0 {
        return KpMember { kpu: raw, in_domain: false, calibrated: true, code: 2 }
    }
    let corr = kpm_pow10(c.pe_i + c.pe_s * (ln(arg) / 2.302585092994046)) / d.fu_p
    return KpMember { kpu: corr, in_domain: ok, calibrated: true, code: 2 }
}

// Declared domain: Schmitt covers every charge class, but its distinctive
// input is a MEASURED membrane affinity.  Imputing log MA from logP makes that
// input a function of one the other members already read, so an imputed MA is
// treated as OUT OF DOMAIN.  That is a conservative choice, not a demonstrated
// necessity -- and the gate cannot tell an imputed value from a measured one
// unless the caller says so truthfully (see T8).
// Measured 2026-10-31 against httk chem.physical_and_invitro.data (18476
// compounds): of the dissertation's 15 drugs only haloperidol (3.723),
// midazolam (2.9) and clozapine (2.954) carry a measured log MA.
pub fn kpm_schmitt_in_domain(d: KpInputs) -> bool {
    if !d.has_ma { return false }
    if d.fu_p <= 0.0 { return false }
    return true
}

// ============================================================================
// MEMBER 2 -- Schmitt + Pearce 2017 regression (the httk default)
// ============================================================================
// Kpu_corr = 10^(a + b*log10(Kpu_raw * fu_p)) / fu_p, muscle coefficients from
// httk::pearce2017regression, column adj.fup (the default path, i.e.
// adjusted.Funbound.plasma = TRUE): a = -0.022, b = 0.658.
// b < 1 is shrinkage, not an offset: it compresses the dynamic range, so the
// correction is largest exactly where the mechanistic prediction is largest.
// This member is CALIBRATED and must never share an ensemble with an
// uncalibrated one.
pub fn kpm_pearce_a_muscle() -> f64 { return 0.0 - 0.022 }
pub fn kpm_pearce_b_muscle() -> f64 { return 0.658 }

pub fn kpm_schmitt_pearce(d: KpInputs) -> KpMember with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let in_dom = kpm_schmitt_in_domain(d)
    let raw = kpm_schmitt_core(d)
    let kp_raw = raw * d.fu_p
    let corr = kpm_pow10(kpm_pearce_a_muscle() + kpm_pearce_b_muscle() * log10(kp_raw)) / d.fu_p
    return KpMember { kpu: corr, in_domain: in_dom, calibrated: true, code: 2 }
}

// ============================================================================
// MEMBER 3 -- Poulin & Theil, via the unified algorithm of Peyret 2010 (Eq. 9)
// ============================================================================
//   Pcw = [ (1 + Im)*Fwm + Pow*Fnlm ] / (1 + Iw)
//   Fwm  = water + 0.70 * neutral phospholipid
//   Fnlm = neutral lipid + 0.30 * neutral phospholipid
// The 0.3 / 0.7 split of neutral phospholipid between the lipid-like and
// water-like phases IS the core of Poulin-Theil.  There is no acidic-
// phospholipid term and no intracellular protein term (Peyret: "Pprw cellular
// = 0 for all compounds"), which is precisely why the method cannot represent
// a strong base.
//
// Peyret is explicit that the VEGETABLE-OIL:water partition is used for
// ADIPOSE and the n-octanol:water partition for every other tissue.  Applying
// the olive-oil correlation to muscle understates the dominant lipid term by
// 7.2x; that error is not made here.
pub fn kpm_poulin_theil(d: KpInputs) -> KpMember with Mut, Div, Panic {
    return KpMember { kpu: kpm_pt_kpu_ph(d, kpm_ph_cell_pt()),
                      in_domain: kpm_pt_in_domain(d), calibrated: false, code: 3 }
}

fn kpm_pt_kpu_ph(d: KpInputs, ph_c: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    kpm_pt_kpu_full(d, ph_c, 1.0, 1.0)
}

// Poulin-Theil takes no acidic-phospholipid multiplier because it has no such
// term: that absence is why a shared [AP-] perturbation moves only Schmitt.
fn kpm_pt_kpu_full(d: KpInputs, ph_c: f64, s_nl: f64, s_np: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let pow_v = kpm_pow10(d.log_p)       // n-octanol:water, muscle

    let im = if d.is_base { kpm_pow10(d.pka - ph_c) } else { 0.0 }
    let iw = if d.is_base { kpm_pow10(d.pka - kpm_ph_plasma()) } else { 0.0 }

    let fwm  = 0.666 + 0.70 * (0.0078 * s_np)
    let fnlm = 0.022 * s_nl + 0.30 * (0.0078 * s_np)
    let pcw = ((1.0 + im) * fwm + pow_v * fnlm) / (1.0 + iw)

    // interstitium: water plus albumin binding (Peyret Eq. 10).  RECONSTRUCTED,
    // not transcribed -- see kpm_pt_oracle_validated().
    let fnlp = 0.0035 * s_nl + 0.30 * (0.00225 * s_np)
    var pprw = (1.0 / d.fu_p - 1.0 - pow_v * fnlp / (1.0 + iw)) / 0.029
    if pprw < 0.0 { pprw = 0.0 }
    let piw = ((1.0 + iw) * 0.89) / (1.0 + iw) + pprw * 0.015

    // Peyret Tab. 2 cellular / interstitial split for muscle
    return pcw * 0.877 + piw * 0.123
}

// Declared domain: neutrals and WEAK bases.  Poulin-Theil has no acidic-
// phospholipid term, so a base that is substantially ionised at intracellular
// pH is outside it; pKa >= 7.0 is the gate.  Of the dissertation's 15 drugs
// this excludes haloperidol, venlafaxine, ODV, olanzapine, clozapine,
// risperidone, 9-OH-risperidone, aripiprazole and quetiapine.
pub fn kpm_pt_in_domain(d: KpInputs) -> bool {
    if d.mw > 700.0 { return false }
    if d.fu_p <= 0.0 { return false }
    if d.is_base {
        if d.pka >= 7.0 { return false }
    }
    return true
}

// Honest provenance flag.  Rodgers-Rowland and both Schmitt members reproduce
// an executable reference; Poulin-Theil does not, because httk implements only
// Schmitt.  Peyret 2010 reproduces each published algorithm at ratio 1 +- 0
// (their Fig. 2), so the equations are source-verified, but the interstitial
// term above is a reconstruction and the cellular / interstitial split uses
// Peyret's rat values over human composition.
pub fn kpm_pt_oracle_validated() -> bool { return false }
pub fn kpm_schmitt_oracle_validated() -> bool { return true }

// ============================================================================
// ENSEMBLE -- fail-closed model-form dispersion
// ============================================================================
// Refusal codes:
//   0  valid
//   1  at least one requested member is out of its declared domain
//   2  fewer than two admitted members -- a dispersion needs two
//   3  calibration status not uniform across the admitted members
//
// dispersion is the sample standard deviation of ln Kpu over the admitted
// members.  It is a DESCRIPTIVE STATISTIC, not a standard uncertainty: the
// members share inputs and composition data, so they are not independent
// draws, and no distribution or dependence model has been stated.  It is 0.0
// whenever the ensemble refuses.  A refusal is not a zero uncertainty: it means
// the statistic is UNAVAILABLE.
struct KpEnsemble {
    dispersion: f64,
    n_admitted:   i32,
    valid:        bool,
    refusal:      i32
}

// want_calibrated selects which homogeneous sub-ensemble to form.  Mixing an
// uncalibrated member with a calibrated one would make the dispersion report
// the calibration rather than the model form, so it is refused rather than
// averaged.
pub fn kpm_ensemble(d: KpInputs, want_calibrated: bool) -> KpEnsemble with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let m0 = kpm_rodgers_rowland(d)
    let m1 = kpm_schmitt_raw(d)
    let m2 = kpm_schmitt_pearce(d)
    let m3 = kpm_poulin_theil(d)

    var n = 0
    var s = 0.0
    var any_out = false

    // member 0
    if m0.calibrated == want_calibrated {
        if m0.in_domain { n = n + 1; s = s + ln(m0.kpu) } else { any_out = true }
    }
    if m1.calibrated == want_calibrated {
        if m1.in_domain { n = n + 1; s = s + ln(m1.kpu) } else { any_out = true }
    }
    if m2.calibrated == want_calibrated {
        if m2.in_domain { n = n + 1; s = s + ln(m2.kpu) } else { any_out = true }
    }
    if m3.calibrated == want_calibrated {
        if m3.in_domain { n = n + 1; s = s + ln(m3.kpu) } else { any_out = true }
    }

    // Fail-closed, B:P inconsistency first: it is a sharper statement than a
    // generic domain miss, because it names WHICH input contradicts WHICH
    // table -- the declared rb against R-R Tab. VII blood-cell composition --
    // so it gets its own code rather than being folded into code 1.  Reported
    // only when R-R would otherwise have been admitted; a compound already
    // outside the structural domain is a code 1, not a code 4.
    if m0.calibrated == want_calibrated {
        if kpm_rr_in_domain(d) {
            if !kpm_rr_bp_consistent(d) {
                return KpEnsemble { dispersion: 0.0, n_admitted: n, valid: false, refusal: 4 }
            }
        }
    }

    // Fail-closed, domain first: an out-of-domain member is a modelling error,
    // not a wide uncertainty, so it is reported even when enough members remain.
    if any_out {
        return KpEnsemble { dispersion: 0.0, n_admitted: n, valid: false, refusal: 1 }
    }
    if n < 2 {
        return KpEnsemble { dispersion: 0.0, n_admitted: n, valid: false, refusal: 2 }
    }

    let mu = s / (n as f64)
    var ss = 0.0
    if m0.calibrated == want_calibrated { if m0.in_domain { let e = ln(m0.kpu) - mu; ss = ss + e * e } }
    if m1.calibrated == want_calibrated { if m1.in_domain { let e = ln(m1.kpu) - mu; ss = ss + e * e } }
    if m2.calibrated == want_calibrated { if m2.in_domain { let e = ln(m2.kpu) - mu; ss = ss + e * e } }
    if m3.calibrated == want_calibrated { if m3.in_domain { let e = ln(m3.kpu) - mu; ss = ss + e * e } }

    let u = kpm_sqrt(ss / ((n - 1) as f64))
    return KpEnsemble { dispersion: u, n_admitted: n, valid: true, refusal: 0 }
}

// ============================================================================
// Reference drug inputs (the three measured witnesses)
// ============================================================================
// haloperidol: logP / pKa / R from R-R 2007 Tab. II; log MA from Schmitt 2008
// Tab. 1 (3.72, matching httk 3.723).  fu_p = 0.10 is the R-R Tab. II value.
// NOTE the open measurand question: haloperidol has FOUR published fu values
// from four measurement bases -- 0.08 (Haase 1981, clinical), 0.10 (R-R 2007),
// 0.1784 (Wetmore 2015, in vitro, via httk) and 0.23 (Schmitt 2008) -- a 2.9x
// range on an input to which Rodgers-Rowland has sensitivity -3.95.  Choosing
// one is a measurand decision, not an estimate; 0.10 is used here because it is
// the value its own method was validated with.
pub fn kpm_drug_haloperidol() -> KpInputs {
    return KpInputs { log_p: 3.61, log_ma: 3.72, has_ma: true, pka: 8.48,
                      is_base: true, fu_p: 0.10, rb: 1.08, mw: 375.86 }
}

// midazolam: logP / pKa / fu from R-R 2007 Tab. III; log MA 2.90 from
// Schmitt 2008 Tab. 1 (matching httk 2.9).  pKa 6.01 < 7, so a weak base and
// inside Poulin-Theil's domain -- this is the in-domain witness.
pub fn kpm_drug_midazolam() -> KpInputs {
    return KpInputs { log_p: 3.15, log_ma: 2.90, has_ma: true, pka: 6.01,
                      is_base: true, fu_p: 0.037, rb: 1.00, mw: 325.77 }
}

// A synthetic neutral at logP 3, to exercise the neutral route with no
// ionisation anywhere.  Not a real drug.  Its log MA is a guess from the
// Schmitt neutral-compound range, so has_ma is FALSE -- an earlier version set
// it true "so the member is admitted", which is exactly the kind of untruthful
// metadata the gate cannot detect.
pub fn kpm_drug_neutral_logp3() -> KpInputs {
    return KpInputs { log_p: 3.00, log_ma: 2.50, has_ma: false, pka: 0.0,
                      is_base: false, fu_p: 0.10, rb: 1.00, mw: 300.0 }
}

// A peptide, to exercise the MW gate: outside Rodgers-Rowland and Poulin-Theil
// by mass and outside Schmitt for want of a measured membrane affinity.
pub fn kpm_drug_peptide() -> KpInputs {
    return KpInputs { log_p: 0.0 - 2.0, log_ma: 0.0, has_ma: false, pka: 0.0,
                      is_base: false, fu_p: 0.01, rb: 1.00, mw: 4813.0 }
}

// ============================================================================
// WHAT THE DISPERSION CAN AND CANNOT SEE
// ============================================================================
// The dispersion statistic is computed over members that read OVERLAPPING
// inputs: the same tissue composition, and -- hidden until measured -- a cell
// pH that each method carries internally (Schmitt 6.81, Rodgers-Rowland and
// Poulin-Theil 7.00, while tissue_composition.sio carries 6.97 for muscle).
// Measured at muscle, haloperidol: that convention alone accounts for 1.51x of
// the Rodgers-Rowland / Schmitt gap, which falls from 9.84x to 6.50x when
// Schmitt is evaluated at Rodgers-Rowland's pH. So the claim that one shared
// input record makes any residual disagreement attributable to the methods is
// FALSE for pH, and the earlier three-member factorial was really a
// four-factor one.
//
// THE METRIC.  Perturb an input, collect the response of every admitted member
// as a vector D of changes in ln Kpu, and ask how much of D lies OUTSIDE the
// direction in which all members move together:
//
//     blindness = || D - mean(D) * 1 || / || D ||
//
// Both extremes are closed forms, which is what makes this a scale:
//   - a perturbation that moves exactly ONE of n members gives
//     sqrt((n-1)/n)  -- 0.707107 for n = 2, 0.816497 for n = 3;
//   - a perturbation that moves every member by the SAME amount in ln Kpu
//     gives 0, because the response is purely uniform.
// Low values mean the dispersion registers little of the input's effect even
// though the predictions themselves move.
//
// A FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS WAS WRONG AND IS RECORDED SO IT IS NOT REPEATED.  The
// version committed in 5748a2719 used |d sd| / |d mean| instead. That ratio is
// exactly sqrt(2) for a one-member perturbation when n = 2, which looked like a
// clean calibration, but it holds ONLY for n = 2: in general the first-order
// value is n*e_k / ((n-1)*sd), which depends on where the perturbed member sits
// relative to the centroid. For n = 3 the perturbed member crosses the centroid
// as delta grows, so the index is non-monotone and does not converge -- measured
// 1.165857, 0.007324, 0.351425 at delta = 0.30, 0.05, 0.01 on the same input.
// The projection form above is delta-independent to six decimals.
//
// MEASURED 2026-11-02, muscle.
//   haloperidol -- the gate admits TWO members (Poulin-Theil is refused):
//     log MA +0.30 / +0.05 / +0.01   0.707107 each   = the one-member reference
//     cell pH +0.40 / +0.10 / +0.02  0.054718 / 0.029139 / 0.023047
//   midazolam -- the gate admits THREE members:
//     log MA +0.30 / +0.05 / +0.01   0.816497 each   = the one-member reference
//     cell pH +0.40 / +0.10 / +0.02  0.807665 / 0.807352 / 0.807227
//
// THE RESULT IS NOT WHAT IT FIRST LOOKED LIKE.  With two members the shared pH
// is nearly invisible to the dispersion; with three it is almost fully visible,
// at 0.807 against a one-member reference of 0.816. So blindness is NOT a
// property of an input being shared. It is a property of the admitted members
// having PROPORTIONAL sensitivities to that input. Rodgers-Rowland and Schmitt
// happen to share the pH response almost exactly -- over 6.60 to 7.40 their
// predictions move 5.19x and 5.72x -- because in both the cation / acidic-
// phospholipid term dominates and scales the same way with pH. Poulin-Theil has
// no such term at all and moves only 1.12x, so adding it makes the response
// non-uniform and the dispersion sees pH again.
//
// AND THAT CUTS AGAINST THE GATE.  By refusing Poulin-Theil for a strong base,
// the domain gate removes the member that would have made pH visible. For a
// strong base it therefore buys robustness against an out-of-domain member --
// the admitted span moves 1.10x over 0.80 pH units, against 4.19x with
// Poulin-Theil admitted -- at the price of blinding the dispersion to the input
// whose provenance is weakest in the composition table (tc_eps_ph = 0.40). The
// gate trades one failure mode for another, and this has to be reported per
// input and per admitted set, not asserted once for the ensemble.
//
// LIMITS.  Two drugs, one tissue, two inputs. Nothing here converts a
// dispersion into a standard uncertainty; it bounds what a dispersion registers.


// ============================================================================
// MEMBER 3b -- Poulin-Theil on PEYRET'S OWN NUMBERS  (added 2026-11-08)
// ============================================================================
// Same equation form as kpm_pt_kpu_full -- Poulin-Theil, so no acidic
// phospholipid term -- with every composition constant taken from Peyret 2010
// Tab. 2 and Tab. 3 instead of the Poulin & Theil 2002 lipid values the
// hybrid member uses.  The point is a controlled comparison: ONE equation,
// TWO tables, so whatever separates the two members is source and not form.
//
// The hybrid member (kpm_poulin_theil) is kept untouched.  It is what the
// existing figures correspond to, and it is not oracle-validated either way
// (kpm_pt_oracle_validated() is false), so neither member is certified and
// silently replacing one with the other would hide that.
//
// COVERAGE IS TWO ORGANS, NOT THREE.  Peyret carries adipose, but the
// Poulin-Theil form needs a VEGETABLE OIL:water partition coefficient there
// rather than n-octanol:water, and no sourced correlation for it exists in
// this repository -- the note at the top of this file mentions one and does
// not implement it.  Supplying it from memory is precisely what this module
// refuses to do, so adipose is out until that correlation is read.
fn kpm_pt_peyret_core(d: KpInputs, organ: i32, ph_c: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let pow_v = kpm_pow10(d.log_p)
    let im = if d.is_base { kpm_pow10(d.pka - ph_c) } else { 0.0 }
    let iw = if d.is_base { kpm_pow10(d.pka - kpm_ph_plasma()) } else { 0.0 }

    // Cells: Fwm = water + 0.70*neutral phospholipid,
    //        Fnlm = neutral lipid + 0.30*neutral phospholipid  (Peyret Eq. 2)
    let fwm  = tc_pey_cw(organ)  + 0.70 * tc_pey_cnpl(organ)
    let fnlm = tc_pey_cnl(organ) + 0.30 * tc_pey_cnpl(organ)
    let pcw = ((1.0 + im) * fwm + pow_v * fnlm) / (1.0 + iw)

    // Interstitium: water plus albumin, the protein term back-calculated from
    // fu_p against Peyret's own plasma row rather than the hybrid's.
    let fnlp = tc_peyret_plasma_nl() + 0.30 * tc_peyret_plasma_npl()
    var pprw = (1.0 / d.fu_p - 1.0 - pow_v * fnlp / (1.0 + iw)) / tc_peyret_plasma_alb()
    if pprw < 0.0 { pprw = 0.0 }
    let piw = tc_pey_iw(organ) + pprw * tc_pey_ialb(organ)

    return pcw * tc_pey_cell(organ) + piw * tc_pey_intf(organ)
}

// in_domain carries BOTH gates: Poulin-Theil's own declared domain (which
// already refuses strong bases at pKa >= 7.0) and the two-organ coverage.
pub fn kpm_pt_peyret_organ(d: KpInputs, organ: i32) -> KpMember with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let ok = if tc_pey_pt_supported(organ) { kpm_pt_in_domain(d) } else { false }
    return KpMember { kpu: kpm_pt_peyret_core(d, organ, kpm_ph_cell_pt()),
                      in_domain: ok, calibrated: false, code: 3 }
}

// Ensemble over an organ.  Only Schmitt has sourced per-organ composition
// today, and its two members sit in DIFFERENT calibration classes, so each
// homogeneous sub-ensemble is a singleton and this refuses with code 2 for
// every supported organ.  That refusal is the honest state of the extension,
// and T17 pins it: extending the composition table to ten organs did NOT
// produce a usable ensemble, and the reason is named rather than averaged
// away.
//
// WHAT IS MISSING, PRECISELY.  Rodgers-Rowland needs per-organ composition in
// its own parameterisation (R-R Tab. VII gives muscle and blood cells only;
// tc_ap_ratio_httk supplies the [AP-] ratio for ten organs but not the water
// and lipid terms), and Poulin-Theil needs the Peyret Tab. 2 cellular /
// interstitial split per organ.  Neither is in hand.  Evaluating either
// equation form on httk composition would be a third source mixed into a
// comparison whose point is that sources disagree -- the -11.52% already
// measured on [AP-] is the warning -- so it is refused instead of improvised.
pub fn kpm_ensemble_organ(d: KpInputs, organ: i32, want_calibrated: bool) -> KpEnsemble with Mut, Div, Panic {
    if !kpm_organ_supported(organ) {
        return KpEnsemble { dispersion: 0.0, n_admitted: 0, valid: false, refusal: 1 }
    }
    let m1 = kpm_schmitt_raw_organ(d, organ)
    let m2 = kpm_schmitt_pearce_organ(d, organ)
    // Member 3b closes the singleton: Schmitt raw and Poulin-Theil-on-Peyret
    // are both UNCALIBRATED, so the uncalibrated sub-ensemble reaches two
    // members wherever Peyret covers the organ and the drug is inside
    // Poulin-Theil's domain.  Today that is liver and muscle.
    let m3 = kpm_pt_peyret_organ(d, organ)

    var n = 0
    var s = 0.0
    var any_out = false
    if m1.calibrated == want_calibrated {
        if m1.in_domain { n = n + 1; s = s + ln(m1.kpu) } else { any_out = true }
    }
    if m2.calibrated == want_calibrated {
        if m2.in_domain { n = n + 1; s = s + ln(m2.kpu) } else { any_out = true }
    }
    // An organ Peyret does not cover is not an out-of-domain MODELLING error
    // for the ensemble as a whole -- it is simply a member that does not
    // exist there -- so it is skipped rather than counted as any_out.
    if m3.calibrated == want_calibrated {
        if tc_pey_pt_supported(organ) {
            if m3.in_domain { n = n + 1; s = s + ln(m3.kpu) } else { any_out = true }
        }
    }
    if any_out {
        return KpEnsemble { dispersion: 0.0, n_admitted: n, valid: false, refusal: 1 }
    }
    if n < 2 {
        return KpEnsemble { dispersion: 0.0, n_admitted: n, valid: false, refusal: 2 }
    }
    let mu = s / (n as f64)
    var ss = 0.0
    if m1.calibrated == want_calibrated { if m1.in_domain { let e = ln(m1.kpu) - mu; ss = ss + e * e } }
    if m2.calibrated == want_calibrated { if m2.in_domain { let e = ln(m2.kpu) - mu; ss = ss + e * e } }
    if m3.calibrated == want_calibrated {
        if tc_pey_pt_supported(organ) { if m3.in_domain { let e = ln(m3.kpu) - mu; ss = ss + e * e } }
    }
    let u = kpm_sqrt(ss / ((n - 1) as f64))
    return KpEnsemble { dispersion: u, n_admitted: n, valid: true, refusal: 0 }
}
// Perturbation codes for kpm_blindness.
//   0  cell pH      -- SHARED: added to each member's own native pH
//   1  log P        -- SHARED
//   2  pKa          -- SHARED
//   3  fu_p         -- SHARED
//   4  log MA       -- read only by Schmitt (the one-member reference case)
//   5  neutral lipid        -- SHARED, relative multiplier
//   6  neutral phospholipid -- SHARED, relative multiplier
//   7  acidic phospholipid  -- SHARED, but Poulin-Theil has no such term and
//                             Rodgers-Rowland depends on it only through the
//                             tissue / blood-cell ratio, so a uniform change
//                             moves ONLY Schmitt
pub fn kpm_pert_ph() -> i32    { return 0 }
pub fn kpm_pert_logp() -> i32  { return 1 }
pub fn kpm_pert_pka() -> i32   { return 2 }
pub fn kpm_pert_fu() -> i32    { return 3 }
pub fn kpm_pert_ma() -> i32    { return 4 }
pub fn kpm_pert_nl() -> i32    { return 5 }
pub fn kpm_pert_np() -> i32    { return 6 }
pub fn kpm_pert_ap() -> i32    { return 7 }

// Closed form for a perturbation only ONE of n admitted members reads:
// the response is delta*e_k, whose uniform component has norm delta/sqrt(n),
// leaving sqrt(1 - 1/n) = sqrt((n-1)/n) outside it.
pub fn kpm_blindness_reference(n: i32) -> f64 with Mut, Div {
    if n < 2 { return 0.0 }
    return kpm_sqrt(((n - 1) as f64) / (n as f64))
}

// Shifted input record for the codes that live in KpInputs.
fn kpm_shift(d: KpInputs, code: i32, delta: f64) -> KpInputs {
    if code == 1 { return KpInputs { log_p: d.log_p + delta, log_ma: d.log_ma, has_ma: d.has_ma,
                                     pka: d.pka, is_base: d.is_base, fu_p: d.fu_p, rb: d.rb, mw: d.mw } }
    if code == 2 { return KpInputs { log_p: d.log_p, log_ma: d.log_ma, has_ma: d.has_ma,
                                     pka: d.pka + delta, is_base: d.is_base, fu_p: d.fu_p, rb: d.rb, mw: d.mw } }
    if code == 3 { return KpInputs { log_p: d.log_p, log_ma: d.log_ma, has_ma: d.has_ma,
                                     pka: d.pka, is_base: d.is_base, fu_p: d.fu_p + delta, rb: d.rb, mw: d.mw } }
    if code == 4 { return KpInputs { log_p: d.log_p, log_ma: d.log_ma + delta, has_ma: d.has_ma,
                                     pka: d.pka, is_base: d.is_base, fu_p: d.fu_p, rb: d.rb, mw: d.mw } }
    return d
}

// How many members the measurement is taken over: admitted and uncalibrated.
pub fn kpm_blindness_n(d: KpInputs) -> i32 with Mut {
    var n = 0
    if kpm_rr_in_domain(d)      { n = n + 1 }
    if kpm_schmitt_in_domain(d) { n = n + 1 }
    if kpm_pt_in_domain(d)      { n = n + 1 }
    return n
}

// One member's response in ln Kpu to the perturbation, or 0.0 when that member
// is not admitted. Domain is judged on the UNPERTURBED record, so a
// perturbation cannot silently change which members are admitted.
fn kpm_response(d: KpInputs, which: i32, code: i32, delta: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let dp  = kpm_shift(d, code, delta)
    let dph = if code == 0 { delta } else { 0.0 }
    // composition codes are RELATIVE multipliers, so 1.0 + delta
    let s_nl = if code == 5 { 1.0 + delta } else { 1.0 }
    let s_np = if code == 6 { 1.0 + delta } else { 1.0 }
    let s_ap = if code == 7 { 1.0 + delta } else { 1.0 }
    if which == 0 {
        if !kpm_rr_in_domain(d) { return 0.0 }
        return ln(kpm_rr_kpu_full(dp, kpm_ph_cell_rr() + dph, s_nl, s_np, s_ap))
             - ln(kpm_rr_kpu_full(d, kpm_ph_cell_rr(), 1.0, 1.0, 1.0))
    }
    if which == 1 {
        if !kpm_schmitt_in_domain(d) { return 0.0 }
        return ln(kpm_schmitt_core_full(dp, kpm_ph_cell_schmitt() + dph, s_nl, s_np, s_ap))
             - ln(kpm_schmitt_core_full(d, kpm_ph_cell_schmitt(), 1.0, 1.0, 1.0))
    }
    if !kpm_pt_in_domain(d) { return 0.0 }
    return ln(kpm_pt_kpu_full(dp, kpm_ph_cell_pt() + dph, s_nl, s_np))
         - ln(kpm_pt_kpu_full(d, kpm_ph_cell_pt(), 1.0, 1.0))
}

// The fraction of the response that lies outside the all-members-together
// direction. Negative sentinel when fewer than two members are admitted, or
// when the perturbation moves nothing at all.
pub fn kpm_blindness(d: KpInputs, code: i32, delta: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let n = kpm_blindness_n(d)
    if n < 2 { return 0.0 - 1.0 }
    let r0 = kpm_response(d, 0, code, delta)
    let r1 = kpm_response(d, 1, code, delta)
    let r2 = kpm_response(d, 2, code, delta)
    let total = r0 * r0 + r1 * r1 + r2 * r2
    if total < 1.0e-24 { return 0.0 - 1.0 }
    // the mean is taken over ADMITTED members only; a non-admitted member
    // contributes 0.0 to both sums and must not dilute the mean
    let mu = (r0 + r1 + r2) / (n as f64)
    var perp = 0.0
    if kpm_rr_in_domain(d)      { let e = r0 - mu; perp = perp + e * e }
    if kpm_schmitt_in_domain(d) { let e = r1 - mu; perp = perp + e * e }
    if kpm_pt_in_domain(d)      { let e = r2 - mu; perp = perp + e * e }
    return kpm_sqrt(perp) / kpm_sqrt(total)
}

// ============================================================================
// Accessors
// ============================================================================
// Struct fields do not cross a module boundary in Madaros (error[E259]), so the
// records above are read through these.  Exposing in_domain and calibrated as
// first-class accessors is deliberate: a consumer that wants only the number
// has to walk past the two flags that say whether the number means anything.

pub fn kpm_member_kpu(m: KpMember) -> f64 { return m.kpu }
pub fn kpm_member_in_domain(m: KpMember) -> bool { return m.in_domain }
pub fn kpm_member_calibrated(m: KpMember) -> bool { return m.calibrated }
pub fn kpm_member_code(m: KpMember) -> i32 { return m.code }

pub fn kpm_ens_dispersion(e: KpEnsemble) -> f64 { return e.dispersion }
pub fn kpm_ens_n(e: KpEnsemble) -> i32 { return e.n_admitted }
pub fn kpm_ens_valid(e: KpEnsemble) -> bool { return e.valid }
pub fn kpm_ens_refusal(e: KpEnsemble) -> i32 { return e.refusal }

// Constructor, so callers outside this module can assemble inputs without
// touching private fields.
pub fn kpm_inputs(log_p: f64, log_ma: f64, has_ma: bool, pka: f64,
                  is_base: bool, fu_p: f64, rb: f64, mw: f64) -> KpInputs {
    return KpInputs { log_p: log_p, log_ma: log_ma, has_ma: has_ma, pka: pka,
                      is_base: is_base, fu_p: fu_p, rb: rb, mw: mw }
}

// Same inputs with the acidic-phospholipid concentrations scaled by a common
// factor, used to witness the exact cancellation documented on
// kpm_rodgers_rowland.  Kept as a separate entry point rather than a field so
// that production callers cannot perturb the table by accident.
pub fn kpm_rr_with_ap_scale(d: KpInputs, s: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    kpm_rr_kpu_full(d, kpm_ph_cell_rr(), 1.0, 1.0, s)
}

// ============================================================================
// Module self-test
// ============================================================================
fn main() -> i32 with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    var fails = 0

    // T1 -- the three uncalibrated members agree closely IN DOMAIN (midazolam).
    // Measured in C++ at muscle: 35.16 / 32.10 / 31.00, spread 1.1x.
    let mdz = kpm_drug_midazolam()
    let e_mdz = kpm_ensemble(mdz, false)
    if e_mdz.valid {
        if e_mdz.n_admitted == 3 {
            if e_mdz.dispersion < 0.20 {
                println("T1 PASS: in-domain ensemble valid, 3 members, dispersion < 0.20 on ln Kpu")
            } else {
                println("T1 FAIL: in-domain dispersion unexpectedly wide"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T1 FAIL: expected 3 admitted members in domain"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T1 FAIL: in-domain ensemble refused"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T2 -- a STRONG base must be refused, because Poulin-Theil cannot
    // represent it.  The refusal must be the domain code, not a wide number.
    let hal = kpm_drug_haloperidol()
    let e_hal = kpm_ensemble(hal, false)
    if !e_hal.valid {
        if e_hal.refusal == 1 {
            if e_hal.dispersion == 0.0 {
                println("T2 PASS: strong base refused fail-closed on domain, dispersion unavailable")
            } else {
                println("T2 FAIL: refused but returned a non-zero u"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T2 FAIL: refused for the wrong reason"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T2 FAIL: strong base admitted -- domain gate not holding"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T3 -- the CALIBRATED sub-ensemble has exactly one member, so no
    // dispersion term exists for it.  This is the honest consequence of
    // refusing to mix calibration statuses.
    let e_cal = kpm_ensemble(mdz, true)
    if !e_cal.valid {
        if e_cal.refusal == 2 {
            println("T3 PASS: calibrated sub-ensemble is a singleton, no term defined")
        } else {
            println("T3 FAIL: calibrated sub-ensemble refused for the wrong reason"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T3 FAIL: singleton ensemble produced a dispersion term"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T4 -- the Pearce regression SHRINKS a large mechanistic prediction.
    // b = 0.658 < 1, so the calibrated member must sit below the raw one for a
    // drug whose raw Kpu is large.  Haloperidol raw Kpu at muscle is ~800.
    let raw = kpm_schmitt_raw(hal)
    let cal = kpm_schmitt_pearce(hal)
    if cal.kpu < raw.kpu {
        if cal.calibrated {
            if !raw.calibrated {
                println("T4 PASS: Pearce member shrinks the raw one and is flagged calibrated")
            } else {
                println("T4 FAIL: raw member flagged calibrated"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T4 FAIL: Pearce member not flagged calibrated"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T4 FAIL: regression did not shrink a large prediction"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T5 -- a UNIFORM error in [AP-] cancels EXACTLY in Rodgers-Rowland.
    // Ka is back-calculated from blood cells using the same table, so Ka
    // carries 1/AP_rbc while the tissue term carries Ka*AP_tissue: a common
    // multiplier cancels algebraically, and only the tissue/blood RATIO
    // (2.42 / 0.57 = 4.25) ever enters.  Asserted numerically, not structurally:
    // scaling both concentrations by 1.10 must leave Kpu bit-identical.
    // Consequence: measuring [AP-] better does NOT improve the prediction if
    // the error is uniform across tissues; only a DIFFERENTIAL error between
    // blood cells and tissue is detectable by this method.
    let ap_base = kpm_rr_with_ap_scale(hal, 1.0)
    let ap_up   = kpm_rr_with_ap_scale(hal, 1.10)
    let ap_dn   = kpm_rr_with_ap_scale(hal, 0.50)
    if kpm_abs(ap_up - ap_base) < 1.0e-12 {
        if kpm_abs(ap_dn - ap_base) < 1.0e-12 {
            if ap_base > 0.0 {
                println("T5 PASS: R-R reads [AP-] only via the tissue/blood-cell ratio (invariance)")
            } else {
                println("T5 FAIL: R-R Kpu non-positive"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T5 FAIL: halving [AP-] moved Kpu -- cancellation not exact"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T5 FAIL: scaling [AP-] by 1.10 moved Kpu"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T6 -- a peptide is outside every member; the ensemble must refuse and
    // must not silently fall back to a single admitted member.
    let pep = kpm_drug_peptide()
    let e_pep = kpm_ensemble(pep, false)
    if !e_pep.valid {
        println("T6 PASS: peptide refused by every member")
    } else {
        println("T6 FAIL: peptide admitted"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T7 -- provenance flags are honest: Schmitt has an executable oracle,
    // Poulin-Theil does not.
    if kpm_schmitt_oracle_validated() {
        if !kpm_pt_oracle_validated() {
            println("T7 PASS: oracle-validation flags reflect what was actually checked")
        } else {
            println("T7 FAIL: Poulin-Theil claims oracle validation it does not have"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T7 FAIL: Schmitt oracle flag lost"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T8 -- the gate TRUSTS declared metadata; it does not authenticate it.
    // Same midazolam inputs, has_ma flipped: with has_ma = true the Schmitt
    // member is admitted and the ensemble is valid; with has_ma = false it is
    // out of domain and the ensemble refuses. Nothing in the numbers tells the
    // two apart, so the refusal is only as truthful as the caller's flag. This
    // is a stated LIMIT of the contract, pinned so it is not forgotten.
    let mdz_imp = KpInputs { log_p: 3.15, log_ma: 2.90, has_ma: false, pka: 6.01,
                             is_base: true, fu_p: 0.037, rb: 1.00, mw: 325.77 }
    let e_true  = kpm_ensemble(mdz, false)
    let e_false = kpm_ensemble(mdz_imp, false)
    if e_true.valid {
        if !e_false.valid {
            if e_false.refusal == 1 {
                println("T8 PASS: admission follows the declared has_ma flag alone (limit of the gate)")
            } else {
                println("T8 FAIL: wrong refusal code when has_ma is false"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T8 FAIL: ensemble admitted an undeclared membrane affinity"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T8 FAIL: declared-measured midazolam refused"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T9 -- the blindness metric against BOTH of its closed forms, at two
    // member counts, and the side effect the domain gate has on it.
    //
    // (a) log MA is read only by Schmitt, so it is the one-member case and must
    //     return sqrt((n-1)/n) exactly: 0.707107 at n = 2, 0.816497 at n = 3.
    //     Matching the closed form at two different n is what licenses the
    //     metric as a scale rather than a number.
    // (b) cell pH is read by every member, but that is NOT sufficient for
    //     blindness. With the two members the gate admits for a strong base the
    //     dispersion registers almost none of it; with the three admitted for a
    //     weak base it registers nearly all of it, because Poulin-Theil has no
    //     cation / acidic-phospholipid term and so does not follow the others.
    //     Blindness tracks PROPORTIONAL sensitivity, not shared inputs.
    // (c) The consequence, asserted here so it cannot be quietly dropped: for a
    //     strong base the gate removes the member that made pH visible.
    let hal_b = kpm_drug_haloperidol()
    let mdz_b = kpm_drug_midazolam()
    let n2 = kpm_blindness_n(hal_b)
    let n3 = kpm_blindness_n(mdz_b)
    let ma2 = kpm_blindness(hal_b, kpm_pert_ma(), 0.30)
    let ma3 = kpm_blindness(mdz_b, kpm_pert_ma(), 0.30)
    let ph2 = kpm_blindness(hal_b, kpm_pert_ph(), 0.40)
    let ph3 = kpm_blindness(mdz_b, kpm_pert_ph(), 0.40)
    println("n (strong base, weak base):")
    println(n2)
    println(n3)
    println("one-member input (log MA): measured, then closed form, at n=2 then n=3:")
    println(ma2)
    println(kpm_blindness_reference(2))
    println(ma3)
    println(kpm_blindness_reference(3))
    println("shared input (cell pH): n=2 then n=3:")
    println(ph2)
    println(ph3)
    if n2 == 2 {
        if n3 == 3 {
            if kpm_abs(ma2 - kpm_blindness_reference(2)) < 1.0e-6 {
                if kpm_abs(ma3 - kpm_blindness_reference(3)) < 1.0e-6 {
                    if ph2 < 0.10 {
                        if ph3 > 0.70 {
                            println("T9 PASS: both closed forms matched; shared pH blind at n=2, visible at n=3")
                        } else {
                            println("T9 FAIL: shared pH not visible at n=3 -- the gate side effect is unproven"); fails = fails + 1
                        }
                    } else {
                        println("T9 FAIL: shared pH not blind at n=2"); fails = fails + 1
                    }
                } else {
                    println("T9 FAIL: one-member case misses the closed form at n=3"); fails = fails + 1
                }
            } else {
                println("T9 FAIL: one-member case misses the closed form at n=2"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T9 FAIL: weak base did not admit three members"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T9 FAIL: strong base did not admit exactly two members"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T10 -- the index refuses rather than returning a misleading number when
    // there is no dispersion to measure. A peptide is out of every domain.
    if kpm_blindness(kpm_drug_peptide(), kpm_pert_ph(), 0.40) < 0.0 {
        println("T10 PASS: blindness refuses when fewer than two members are admitted")
    } else {
        println("T10 FAIL: blindness returned a value with no admitted ensemble"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T11 -- the same metric on the TISSUE COMPOSITION, which refutes the
    // tempting generalisation that a dispersion is blind to shared inputs.
    //
    // Measured at muscle, both engines, delta-independent to six decimals:
    //                      haloperidol (n=2)   midazolam (n=3)
    //   cell pH                 0.054718            0.807665
    //   neutral lipid           0.645984            0.235045
    //   neutral phospholipid    0.773990            0.409417
    //   acidic phospholipid     0.707107            0.816497
    //
    // Only cell pH is invisible, and only at n = 2. The composition fractions
    // are all registered. So blindness is NOT a property of sharing an input:
    // pH is special because both methods route it through the same
    // Henderson-Hasselbalch factor on the dominant term, giving near-identical
    // log-responses, while the composition fractions enter with method-specific
    // weights (Rodgers-Rowland f_nl 0.022 against Schmitt 0.02387 * 0.5394 =
    // 0.01288) and so move the members by different amounts.
    //
    // That sharpens the pH result rather than weakening it: the one shared
    // input a two-member dispersion cannot see is also the one carrying the
    // lowest evidence weight in the composition table (tc_eps_ph = 0.40).
    //
    // The acidic-phospholipid row is a CLOSED-FORM PREDICTION, and it is the
    // reason this test is worth having. A uniform [AP-] change moves only
    // Schmitt -- Rodgers-Rowland depends on it through the tissue / blood-cell
    // ratio alone (T5), and Poulin-Theil has no acidic-phospholipid term at all
    // -- so it must land exactly on the one-member reference at BOTH member
    // counts, by two different mechanisms: cancellation in the strong-base
    // route, absence in the weak-base route and in Poulin-Theil. The metric
    // therefore rediscovers T5's invariance from the outside.
    let ap2 = kpm_blindness(hal_b, kpm_pert_ap(), 0.10)
    let ap3 = kpm_blindness(mdz_b, kpm_pert_ap(), 0.10)
    let nl2 = kpm_blindness(hal_b, kpm_pert_nl(), 0.10)
    let np2 = kpm_blindness(hal_b, kpm_pert_np(), 0.10)
    println("composition, n=2: [AP-] / neutral lipid / neutral phospholipid:")
    println(ap2)
    println(nl2)
    println(np2)
    println("[AP-] at n=3 (expect the n=3 one-member reference):")
    println(ap3)
    if kpm_abs(ap2 - kpm_blindness_reference(2)) < 1.0e-6 {
        if kpm_abs(ap3 - kpm_blindness_reference(3)) < 1.0e-6 {
            if nl2 > 0.30 {
                if np2 > 0.30 {
                    println("T11 PASS: [AP-] hits both one-member forms; composition is NOT blind")
                } else {
                    println("T11 FAIL: neutral phospholipid unexpectedly blind at n=2"); fails = fails + 1
                }
            } else {
                println("T11 FAIL: neutral lipid unexpectedly blind at n=2"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T11 FAIL: [AP-] misses the one-member form at n=3"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T11 FAIL: [AP-] misses the one-member form at n=2"); fails = fails + 1
    }


    // T12 -- B:P CONSISTENCY OF THE STRONG-BASE ROUTE (audit 2026-11-02).
    // Three claims, each measured in scratchpad/audit1/bp1.cpp and bp2.cpp:
    //  (a) the dissertation set is B:P-consistent, venlafaxine by the thinnest
    //      margin in the set (+0.1265 on rb, i.e. +12.7%);
    //  (b) an rb below the critical value is REFUSED, with the dedicated code
    //      4, instead of being clamped to Ka = 0 and returned silently;
    //  (c) the weak-base route never reads rb, so it is reported as trivially
    //      consistent rather than carrying a fabricated margin.
    // Before the fix (b) failed: in_domain stayed true and the ensemble
    // returned a dispersion computed from a Kpu whose implied B:P was 0.873
    // against an input of 0.56.
    let t12_vfx = KpInputs { log_p: 3.20, log_ma: 0.0, has_ma: false, pka: 9.40,
                             is_base: true, fu_p: 0.73, rb: 1.00, mw: 277.40 }
    let t12_bad = KpInputs { log_p: 3.20, log_ma: 0.0, has_ma: false, pka: 9.40,
                             is_base: true, fu_p: 0.73, rb: 0.56, mw: 277.40 }
    let t12_weak = KpInputs { log_p: 3.00, log_ma: 0.0, has_ma: false, pka: 6.01,
                              is_base: true, fu_p: 0.032, rb: 1.00, mw: 325.77 }

    let mg_vfx = kpm_rr_bp_margin(t12_vfx)
    let mg_bad = kpm_rr_bp_margin(t12_bad)

    // (a) venlafaxine consistent, and the margin is the measured one.
    if kpm_rr_bp_consistent(t12_vfx) {
        if mg_vfx > 0.12 {
            if mg_vfx < 0.13 {
                println("T12a PASS: venlafaxina consistente, margem medida em rb")
            } else {
                println("T12a FAIL: margem da venlafaxina acima de 0.13"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T12a FAIL: margem da venlafaxina abaixo de 0.12"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T12a FAIL: venlafaxina marcada inconsistente"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // (b) low rb is refused as code 4, and the member is out of domain.
    if mg_bad < 0.0 {
        if kpm_rr_bp_consistent(t12_bad) {
            println("T12b FAIL: rb abaixo do critico aceito como consistente"); fails = fails + 1
        } else {
            let m_bad = kpm_rodgers_rowland(t12_bad)
            if kpm_member_in_domain(m_bad) {
                println("T12b FAIL: membro R-R ficou in_domain com B:P inconsistente"); fails = fails + 1
            } else {
                let e_bad = kpm_ensemble(t12_bad, false)
                if kpm_ens_valid(e_bad) {
                    println("T12b FAIL: ensemble valido com B:P inconsistente"); fails = fails + 1
                } else {
                    if kpm_ens_refusal(e_bad) == 4 {
                        println("T12b PASS: rb sub-critico recusado com codigo 4, nao clampado")
                    } else {
                        println("T12b FAIL: codigo de recusa errado para B:P inconsistente"); fails = fails + 1
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    } else {
        println("T12b FAIL: rb 0.56 nao produziu margem negativa"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // (c) the weak-base route does not read rb.
    if kpm_rr_bp_consistent(t12_weak) {
        if kpm_rr_bp_margin(t12_weak) == 0.0 {
            println("T12c PASS: rota de base fraca nao le rb; margem reportada como 0.0")
        } else {
            println("T12c FAIL: margem fabricada para a rota de base fraca"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T12c FAIL: base fraca marcada inconsistente"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T13 -- CERTIFICATION OF THE PER-ORGAN TABLE.  Organ 6 through the
    // generalised path must reproduce the muscle-only path bit for bit.  If
    // the table were mistranscribed this is what catches it, and nothing else
    // downstream would be trustworthy.
    let t13_hal = KpInputs { log_p: 4.30, log_ma: 3.723, has_ma: true, pka: 8.66,
                             is_base: true, fu_p: 0.10, rb: 1.08, mw: 375.86 }
    let old_mus = kpm_schmitt_core_full(t13_hal, kpm_ph_cell_schmitt(), 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
    let new_mus = kpm_schmitt_core_organ(t13_hal, 6, kpm_ph_cell_schmitt(), 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
    if old_mus == new_mus {
        println("T13 PASS: organo 6 pela tabela reproduz o caminho hardcoded bit a bit")
    } else {
        println("T13 FAIL: tabela do musculo divergiu do caminho hardcoded")
        print("  hardcoded = "); println(old_mus)
        print("  tabela    = "); println(new_mus)
        fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T14 -- bone is refused, and the reason is a datum.
    if kpm_organ_supported(10) {
        println("T14 FAIL: osso admitido apesar de nao fechar"); fails = fails + 1
    } else {
        let m_bone = kpm_schmitt_raw_organ(t13_hal, 10)
        if kpm_member_in_domain(m_bone) {
            println("T14 FAIL: membro do osso ficou in_domain"); fails = fails + 1
        } else {
            if kpm_organ_bone_closure() < 0.50 {
                println("T14 PASS: osso recusado, fechamento 0.49 registrado como dado")
            } else {
                println("T14 FAIL: fechamento do osso nao registrado"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        }
    }

    // T15 -- coverage: exactly ten organs supported, and the refused ones are
    // plasma (0), bone (10), pancreas (12) and other (13).
    var sup = 0
    var oi = 0
    while oi < 14 {
        if kpm_organ_supported(oi) { sup = sup + 1 }
        oi = oi + 1
    }
    if sup == kpm_organ_count() {
        if sup == 10 {
            println("T15 PASS: 10 orgaos suportados; plasma, osso, pancreas e other recusados")
        } else {
            print("T15 FAIL: contagem inesperada = "); println(sup); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T15 FAIL: kpm_organ_count nao casa com o gate"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T16 -- Pearce shrinks in every supported organ (b < 1 everywhere), so a
    // large raw prediction must come down in all ten, not just in muscle.
    var shrunk = 0
    var checked = 0
    var oj = 1
    while oj < 12 {
        if kpm_organ_supported(oj) {
            checked = checked + 1
            let mr = kpm_schmitt_raw_organ(t13_hal, oj)
            let mp = kpm_schmitt_pearce_organ(t13_hal, oj)
            if kpm_member_kpu(mp) < kpm_member_kpu(mr) { shrunk = shrunk + 1 }
        }
        oj = oj + 1
    }
    if checked == 10 {
        if shrunk == 10 {
            println("T16 PASS: Pearce encolhe a predicao bruta nos 10 orgaos")
        } else {
            print("T16 FAIL: encolheu em apenas "); println(shrunk); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        print("T16 FAIL: orgaos verificados = "); println(checked); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T17 -- UPDATED 2026-11-08.  Until member 3b existed this asserted ten
    // organs and ten refusals, because only Schmitt had per-organ
    // composition and its two members sit in different calibration classes.
    // Poulin-Theil-on-Peyret closes the singleton in the two organs Peyret
    // covers, so the count is now eight refusals and two valid.  The old
    // assertion failed the moment the member was wired in, which is the test
    // doing its job rather than a regression.
    var refused = 0
    var wrong_code = 0
    var ok_organs = 0
    var ok = 1
    while ok < 12 {
        if kpm_organ_supported(ok) {
            ok_organs = ok_organs + 1
            let e = kpm_ensemble_organ(t13_hal, ok, false)
            if kpm_ens_valid(e) {
                wrong_code = wrong_code + 1
            } else {
                if kpm_ens_refusal(e) == 2 { refused = refused + 1 } else { wrong_code = wrong_code + 1 }
            }
        }
        ok = ok + 1
    }
    if ok_organs == 10 {
        if refused == 8 {
            if wrong_code == 2 {
                println("T17 PASS: 8 recusas codigo 2 e 2 validos -- PT-Peyret fechou o singleton")
            } else {
                print("T17 FAIL: validos = "); println(wrong_code); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            print("T17 FAIL: recusas = "); println(refused); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T17 FAIL: contagem de orgaos"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T18 -- an unsupported organ refuses on DOMAIN (code 1), distinguishable
    // from the singleton refusal above.  Bone and pancreas must not look the
    // same as "not enough members".
    let e_bone = kpm_ensemble_organ(t13_hal, 10, false)
    let e_panc = kpm_ensemble_organ(t13_hal, 12, false)
    if kpm_ens_refusal(e_bone) == 1 {
        if kpm_ens_refusal(e_panc) == 1 {
            println("T18 PASS: orgao nao suportado recusa por dominio (1), nao por contagem (2)")
        } else {
            println("T18 FAIL: pancreas com codigo errado"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T18 FAIL: osso com codigo errado"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T19 -- PT-on-Peyret: coverage is two organs, and adipose is refused for
    // the vegetable-oil:water correlation rather than for missing composition.
    // Peyret HAS adipose; the repository has no sourced Pvo:w.
    var pn = 0
    var pq = 0
    while pq < 14 {
        if tc_pey_pt_supported(pq) { pn = pn + 1 }
        pq = pq + 1
    }
    if pn == tc_pey_pt_count() {
        if pn == 2 {
            if tc_pey_pt_supported(7) {
                println("T19 FAIL: adiposo admitido sem correlacao oleo vegetal:agua"); fails = fails + 1
            } else {
                println("T19 PASS: PT-Peyret cobre figado e musculo; adiposo recusado por falta de Pvo:w")
            }
        } else {
            print("T19 FAIL: cobertura PT-Peyret = "); println(pn); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T19 FAIL: contagem PT-Peyret inconsistente"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T20 -- ONE equation, TWO tables.  The hybrid and the pure-Peyret member
    // differ on muscle, and the difference is source, not form: the equation
    // is identical and only the composition constants change.  Peyret's cell
    // neutral lipid is 55% lower, so for a lipophilic base the Peyret member
    // must come out BELOW the hybrid.
    let t20_mid = KpInputs { log_p: 3.00, log_ma: 2.9, has_ma: true, pka: 6.01,
                             is_base: true, fu_p: 0.032, rb: 1.00, mw: 325.77 }
    let hyb = kpm_poulin_theil(t20_mid)
    let pey = kpm_pt_peyret_organ(t20_mid, 6)
    if kpm_member_in_domain(hyb) {
        if kpm_member_in_domain(pey) {
            if kpm_member_kpu(pey) < kpm_member_kpu(hyb) {
                println("T20 PASS: mesma equacao, duas tabelas -- Peyret abaixo do hibrido no musculo")
            } else {
                println("T20 FAIL: Peyret nao ficou abaixo do hibrido"); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T20 FAIL: membro Peyret fora de dominio no musculo"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T20 FAIL: membro hibrido fora de dominio"); fails = fails + 1
    }

    // T21 -- THE ENSEMBLE FINALLY VALIDATES, and only where it should.  With
    // Schmitt raw and PT-on-Peyret both uncalibrated, the uncalibrated
    // sub-ensemble reaches two members in liver and muscle.  Everywhere else
    // it still refuses with code 2.  This is the first configuration in this
    // module whose dispersion measures model form rather than source
    // disagreement: both members read one composition table each, and neither
    // is a hybrid.
    let e_liv = kpm_ensemble_organ(t20_mid, 1, false)
    let e_mus = kpm_ensemble_organ(t20_mid, 6, false)
    let e_kid = kpm_ensemble_organ(t20_mid, 2, false)
    if kpm_ens_valid(e_liv) {
        if kpm_ens_valid(e_mus) {
            if kpm_ens_n(e_mus) == 2 {
                if kpm_ens_valid(e_kid) {
                    println("T21 FAIL: rim validou sem membro Peyret"); fails = fails + 1
                } else {
                    if kpm_ens_refusal(e_kid) == 2 {
                        println("T21 PASS: ensemble valido em figado e musculo (n=2); recusa 2 nos demais")
                    } else {
                        println("T21 FAIL: codigo de recusa errado no rim"); fails = fails + 1
                    }
                }
            } else {
                print("T21 FAIL: n no musculo = "); println(kpm_ens_n(e_mus)); fails = fails + 1
            }
        } else {
            println("T21 FAIL: musculo nao validou"); fails = fails + 1
        }
    } else {
        println("T21 FAIL: figado nao validou"); fails = fails + 1
    }
    if fails == 0 {
        println("PASS")
        return 0
    }
    println("FAIL")
    return 1
}
